From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 08:44:20 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 163/342] meson_ir, Remote Control: infrared remotes as
 keystrokes

The ODROID-C2's infrared receiver (the S905's multi-format IR decoder
at 0xc8100580) had no driver.

- meson_ir (drivers/input) runs the receiver in raw mode: an interrupt
  at every edge with the time since the one before, in 10 us ticks
  (docs/m24-ir-facts.md). NEC (with extended addresses and NEC32), RC-5
  and RC-5X, RC-6 mode 0, Sony 12/15/20 and JVC are decoded in software
  (IRDecoder.cpp, also built on the build host by
  tools/tests/irdecoder); a timer ends frames whose last space only the
  next edge would measure. It publishes /dev/input/remote/meson_ir/0:
  reads give decoded presses (ir_remote.h) to every open handle at its
  own pace; ioctls give the raw pulses for learning, counters, and a
  wait with a timeout.
- The input_server add-on "Remote Control" looks each press up in the
  enabled profiles and sends its keystroke through the current keymap,
  as a keyboard would; media key codes work as the HID driver's. A held
  button keeps its key down until the remote stops repeating, and can
  repeat it. While the preferences learn, presses go to them.
- The Remote Control preferences: profiles (new, duplicate, delete,
  import, export), a table of buttons with their keystrokes (pressed
  into a field, or picked by name), Learn, and templates for
  MediaPlayer, a music player, desktop navigation and the system volume.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
